---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

import '../assets/brand/tokens.css';
import '../styles/site.css';
import heroWide from '../assets/brand/diagrams/checkout-example.svg?raw';
import heroNarrow from '../assets/brand/diagrams/checkout-example-narrow.svg?raw';

const title = 'Waggle: when contributors run agents, the agents collide';
const description =
  'Several people, each running an AI coding agent, means overlapping edits, duplicated work, and conflicting pull requests. Waggle is a hive for them: each keeper and their queen get a claim for every task, and a gate on GitHub blocks any merge outside it.';
const site = import.meta.env.SITE;
const canonical = site ? new URL('/', site).href : undefined;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    {canonical && <link rel="canonical" href={canonical} />}
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/brand/logo/w-mark-favicon-light.svg" type="image/svg+xml" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/brand/logo/w-mark-favicon-dark.svg" type="image/svg+xml" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png" />
  </head>
  <body class="lp">
    <div class="lp-wrap">
      <nav class="lp-nav" aria-label="Main">
        <a class="mark" href="/">
          <img class="mark-img light-only" src="/brand/logo/w-mark-light.svg" alt="" width="24" height="24" />
          <img class="mark-img dark-only" src="/brand/logo/w-mark-dark.svg" alt="" width="24" height="24" />
          Waggle
        </a>
        <a href="/docs/">Docs</a>
        <a href="/docs/quick-start/">Quick start</a>
        <a href="/docs/gate/">Gate</a>
        <a href="/docs/claims-and-leases/">Claims</a>
        <a href="/docs/config/">Config</a>
        <a href="/docs/cli/">CLI</a>
        <a href="/docs/recipes/">Recipes</a>
      </nav>

      <header class="lp-hero">
        <img
          class="walt"
          src="/brand/mascot/walt-vector-normalized.svg"
          width="200"
          height="160"
          alt="Walt, the Waggle mascot: a round apricot bee with two dark stripes, soft wings and a small smile."
        />
        <div class="lp-hero-copy">
        <h1>When contributors run agents, the agents collide.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Overlapping edits, the same work done twice, pull requests that conflict, and nobody who can say who is on what.
          Waggle is a hive for many people and their agent teams on one repository. Each keeper (a human in charge) talks with a
          queen (an agent that plans the work). She writes each task a lane, called a claim, and the gate fails a merge that leaves it.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-note">The gate works today. Nectar and Quorum are on <code>main</code> but not in a release yet, and each part of the hive says where it stands.</p>
        <div class="lp-actions">
          <a class="lp-btn primary" href="/docs/">Read the docs</a>
          <a class="lp-btn" href="/docs/quick-start/">Quick start</a>
          <a class="lp-btn" href="/docs/hive/">Meet the hive</a>
        </div>
        </div>
      </header>

      <section aria-labelledby="how">
        <h2 id="how">How it works, with an example</h2>
        <p>
          A forager (the coding agent doing the work) opens a pull request. The gate, our merge check, reads the lease (the
          directories the task may change) and tests every commit against it. One commit outside the lease blocks the merge.
        </p>
      </section>
      <figure class="lp-diagram" style="margin-inline: 0">
        <div class="wide" set:html={heroWide} />
        <div class="narrow" set:html={heroNarrow} />
      </figure>

      <section aria-labelledby="hive">
        <h2 id="hive">Meet the hive</h2>
        <p>
          Waggle talks in bees on purpose. Every role is a plain job underneath, so here is each one in a line. Most of the work
          comes from the agent team: keepers talk with their queens, and the queens open the GitHub issues and write the claims.
          A person can also write an issue.
        </p>
        <div class="lp-roles-grid">
          <article class="lp-role">
            <h3>Keeper</h3>
            <p>A human in charge. You own a plan and the agents that carry it out, and you stay the one who decides.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="lp-role">
            <h3>Queen</h3>
            <p>An agent you talk with. She plans the work, opens the issue and writes the claim. She does not write feature code.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="lp-role">
            <h3>Forager</h3>
            <p>The coding agent doing the work. It goes out, changes the files and brings a pull request back. It never writes its own claim.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="lp-role">
            <h3>Guard</h3>
            <p>The reviewer that checks the work. It reviews the pull request and says pass or must-fix. <span class="lp-tag live">Built</span></p>
          </article>
          <article class="lp-role">
            <h3>Builder</h3>
            <p>A worker that implements a task inside its claim. <code>waggle dispatch</code> runs one today. <span class="lp-tag live">Built</span></p>
          </article>
          <article class="lp-role">
            <h3>Scout</h3>
            <p>A planner that searches ahead and writes the task brief. The instructions ship, but Waggle does not run scouts for you yet. <span class="lp-tag soon">Coming soon</span></p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <p>
          <a href="/docs/hive/">The whole hive, built and coming</a> &middot; <a href="/docs/glossary/">Glossary of every bee word</a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="pains">
        <h2 id="pains">What goes wrong without a hive</h2>
        <div class="lp-grid">
          <div>
            <h3>Overlapping edits</h3>
            <p>Your agents and a teammate's agents change the same files. One side overwrites the other, or the pull requests will not merge.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>The same work, twice</h3>
            <p>Nothing shared records that a task is already taken, so another contributor's agent starts it and the effort is wasted.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Nobody can see who is on what</h3>
            <p>Each contributor's agents cannot see the others'. Branches pile up, and a keeper has to reconstruct who is on what from the diffs.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="fix">
        <h2 id="fix">The first piece you can use today: the gate</h2>
        <div class="lp-grid">
          <div>
            <h3>A claim</h3>
            <p>
              A record of one task: the directories it may change and the branches its pull request may come from. You talk
              with your queen and she writes the claim. The forager doing the work never writes its own. A queen opening
              issues straight from your conversation is <span class="lp-tag soon">Coming soon</span>; a person can also write an issue.
              <a href="/docs/claims-and-leases/">Claims and leases</a>
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>A lease</h3>
            <p>Those directories. That is the lane the forager is supposed to stay inside, like a bee on its own comb.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>The gate</h3>
            <p>
              A GitHub merge check, <code>verify-leases-and-tiers</code>. It fails a pull request whose commits change a path outside the lease, or whose claim
              does not name that pull request's branch. It checks every commit, not only the final diff.
              <a href="/docs/gate/">The gate spec</a>
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>A keeper stays in control</h3>
            <p>
              A keeper is the person who works with the queen. Paths you list as protected still need an approver's approval.
              Control files such as workflows fail no matter what the claim says. Waggle does not run your foragers; it guards the hive they work in.
              <a href="/docs/gate/#5-risk-tiers-and-protected-paths">Protected paths</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="who">
        <div class="lp-who">
          <img
            class="walt-small"
            src="/brand/mascot/walt-vector-normalized.svg"
            width="100"
            height="80"
            alt="Walt the bee."
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <div>
            <h2 id="who">Who it's for</h2>
            <p>
              Teams, and open-source projects, where more than one keeper runs an agent team on the same repository. Today
              the gate holds the line for all of them. Next comes
              <strong>honey</strong>: a contributor's standing, earned from merged work that lasts, with Nectar (reputation)
              and Quorum (voting on priorities). Both are <span class="lp-tag soon">On main</span>, not in a release yet.
              If that sounds like your hive, the <a href="/docs/quick-start/">quick start</a> is the place to begin.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="lp-limits">
      <div class="lp-wrap">
        <h2>What it does not do</h2>
        <p>
          It does not schedule or run foragers, and it is not a sandbox. It binds only on PRs that must pass the check, an
          admin bypass merges without it, and the required check can be forged by a workflow or token with the right
          permissions. The limits are written down, with the tests that show them, in
          <a href="/docs/gate/#10-limits-and-known-bypasses">the gate spec</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Waggle is pre-1.0 and supports GitHub only. The spec describes what the code does today, and the recipes have not
          been run end to end with each tool.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <footer class="lp-wrap lp-foot">
      <p><a href="/docs/why-waggle/">Why Waggle: questions from teams</a></p>
      <p>The <code>waggle</code> CLI is Apache-2.0. The templates <code>waggle init</code> writes into your repo are MIT-0.</p>
      <p>Copyright 2026 Level 19 Enterprises, LLC.</p>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
